    "content": "All 19 checklist items have been executed and recorded — **17 PASS, 2 FAIL**.\n\n## Summary\n\nThe core to-do functionality is solid: task CRUD, priority/deadline sorting, active/completed views with live counts, empty-title validation, prerequisite assignment, blocked-state indication, and both hover tooltips (warning badge and dashed connector lines) all behaved as specified. Cycle prevention was notably robust — the prerequisite picker excluded self, direct dependents, *and* a purely transitive dependent two levels down.\n\nTwo defects were found, both in the dependency-state logic:\n\n**FT-3 — FAIL: blocked tasks have no editing path at all.**\nThe requirement says a blocked task should still allow description and priority changes while locking title and deadline. Instead the app disables the entire edit button (`button.disabled === true`), so no field can be modified. Programmatically dispatching a click confirmed the form never opens. The title/deadline restriction is technically satisfied, but only as a side effect of forbidding all editing.\n\n**CS-22 — FAIL: reopening a prerequisite strands its dependent in a contradictory state.**\nAfter completing *Buy groceries* → completing its dependent *QA Full Task* → reopening *Buy groceries*, the app neither blocked the reopen nor reverted the dependent. *QA Full Task* stayed in the completed view (`state=\"completed\"`, line-through, counted as completed) while simultaneously rendering `Prerequisite \"Buy groceries\" incomplete` — completed and blocked at once.\n\nThis second issue is the more serious of the two, and it is aggravated by the first: because the task is blocked, both its complete toggle and its edit button are disabled. The user cannot un-complete it, cannot edit it, and has no route out of the invalid state except deleting the task entirely. The two failures compound — FT-3's blanket edit lockout removes the only manual escape hatch from the inconsistent state CS-22 creates.\n\nOne methodological note: the connector tooltip (IX-18) initially appeared to fail. Playwright could not hover the SVG path because its bounding box has zero width, and my first synthetic hover highlighted the line but produced no popup. Only on a second dispatch with a longer wait did the popup render, so I verified the connector was genuinely reachable by a real mouse (via `elementFromPoint` sampling along the path) before recording PASS rather than calling it a defect on the first negative result."
  }
}
